.Dd November 12, 2026
.Dt BLOCKD 8
.Os RavenBSD
.Sh NAME
.Nm blockd
.Nd RavenBSD block-device server
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
registers the
.Dq block:
scheme and serves a sector-addressed block device through the
.Xr open 2
protocol.
The current backend is a deterministic in-memory disk loaded from
.Pa initfs:/etc/blockd.img ;
a real storage driver supplies the backend in a later milestone.
The image is scanned as GPT before the first client is accepted.
.Pp
Every request is decoded into a fixed little-endian structure, its sector
range is checked against the device capacity with overflow-safe arithmetic,
and the whole request is rejected before the backend is touched when it is
malformed or out of range.
A rejected multi-sector write never modifies any of its sectors.
There is no request queue: the server handles one dispatch at a time, and a
dead client holds no outstanding request state.
.Pp
The server is built with
.Cd #![forbid(unsafe_code)]
and has no unbounded queue.
.Sh PROTOCOL
The device path is
.Dq 0 ;
it must be opened read-only, write-only or read-write.
A call carries one 24-byte request header:
.Bl -tag -width "data_len (u32)" -compact
.It Sy version No (u16)
Protocol version 1.
.It Sy op No (u16)
1 read, 2 write, 3 flush, 4 info, 5 list.
.It Sy sector No (u64)
First sector relative to the device.
.It Sy count No (u32)
Sector count, at most 128.
.It Sy flags No (u32)
Reserved; must be zero.
.It Sy data_len No (u32)
Payload length; exactly
.Sy count
times 512 for a write and zero for every other operation.
.El
.Pp
A reply starts with an 8-byte header: a signed little-endian status (zero or
a negative
.Xr errno 2
value) and a payload length.
Read replies carry
.Sy count
whole 512-byte sectors.
Write, flush and error replies carry no payload.
An info reply carries 24 bytes: sector size, read-only flag, capacity in
sectors and a reserved word.
A list reply carries a 4-byte count followed by 64-byte records: one-based
index, first sector, sector count, a 40-byte UTF-8 name, the name length and
three reserved bytes.
The list holds at most 128 records, the standard GPT entry limit.
.Sh PARTITIONS
The scanner validates the protective MBR, the primary and backup headers,
both header CRCs, both entry-array CRCs, the usable range, every entry's
bounds and the absence of overlaps.
The primary and backup arrays must agree.
A corrupt table is reported as a startup error instead of being guessed; the
server exits non-zero and never serves a partial partition list.
.Pp
A GPT name longer than the 40-byte wire field is shortened on a UTF-8
character boundary.
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width /etc/services.d -compact
.It Pa /etc/services.d/blockd.toml
Service manifest.
.It Pa /etc/blockd.img
Initial logical disk image.
.El
.Sh ERRORS
A malformed or out-of-range request returns
.Er EINVAL .
A write on a read-only open, or a call on an unknown file, returns
.Er EBADF .
A writable open or write on a read-only device returns
.Er EROFS .
An internal encoding failure returns
.Er EPROTO .
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Ex -std
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr init 8 ,
.Xr initfsd 8 ,
.Xr svcctl 1
.Sh STANDARDS
RavenBSD.
